From a Physical AI perspective, this mechanism ensures that microstructural information is not treated as a fixed diagnostic snapshot but as an evolving probabilistic input that directly influences micromechanical modelling and optimization. For example, clustering of weak ITZ regions extracted from CT gradients is embedded into stochastic crack initiation models, while fiber misalignment distributions contribute to the anisotropic stiffness tensors used in Sect.  3.2 . High fidelity 3D reconstructions such as those shown in Fig.  5  serve as auxiliary but computationally demanding outputs. They are typically executed on external HPC resources or multiple GPU workstations, whereas the MAS workflow relies on distilled descriptors for real-time adaptability. This dual tier approach allows the system to balance computational efficiency with data fidelity, ensuring that vision driven evidence directly informs entropy-based design optimization. Thus, the feedback loop transforms micro-CT from a passive imaging modality into an active computational driver for seismic resilient FRC design. The feedback loop is summarized in Table  10 . Table 10 Dual feedback loop between A3 and A2 Stage Operation Agent responsibility Integration into MAS Descriptor extraction Segmentation of fibers, aggregates, ITZ, pores from micro-CT datasets A3 (3D U-Net CNN + post-processing) Produces raw geometric and topological microstructural data Uncertainty quantification Confidence maps (softmax), ensemble variance, boundary sensitivity metrics A3 Flags low-confidence zones, triggering adaptive re-segmentation if needed Stochastic state vector formation Assembly of ξ  =  φ f , a ij , φ a , φ v , δ ITZ , ⋯ \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
